## Issues

### Bloat findings (this brevity sweep)

- [ ] `description` is ~289 chars (cap 200) and packs three sentences into one
  block: the two delivery channels, the two install entry points, and a
  parenthetical about the install service. Trim to a single ≤200-char sentence
  naming the trunk's two delivery channels.
  Traces to: `description` field | Brevity Contract caps
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity

- [ ] `intent` is ~137 words (cap 100). Compresses fine — the second half
  describing per-tool teaching weight is Strategy material, not intent.
  Traces to: `intent` field | Brevity Contract caps
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity

- [ ] `outcomes` total has 4 desired + ~6 undesired, but each item is paragraph
  length. Outcome 5 ("install action turns a cold host project AIDE-ready")
  enumerates pointer-stub, doc-set, slash-commands, MCP wiring, IDE wiring as
  a phase list — that is implementation contract, not domain success criterion.
  Traces to: `outcomes` items | Brevity Contract per-item ≤2 sentences + outcomes-purpose test
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity

- [ ] `## Context` is ~600 words (cap ~150). Roughly two-thirds of it explains
  brain-layer doctrine: brain-config-as-user-owned, six body sections, sync
  contract, four-state vocabulary, drift-as-hard-halt, `.aide/config/`
  ownership boundary. All of this duplicates content already in the (rewritten)
  root and in the brain-related submodule specs. Per the sibling-redundancy
  doctrine, push it to the brain layer's own parent and prune from the trunk.
  Traces to: `## Context` | Brevity Contract caps + Sibling-redundancy doctrine
  Misalignment: spec-bloat, spec-gap
  Category: Brevity, Sibling-redundancy

- [ ] `## Strategy` has 14+ paragraph-length decisions (cap ~5, ≤80 words each):
  tool-list-as-teaching-artifact, uneven teaching weight, docs-as-source-of-truth,
  progressive disclosure at file layer, no-tool-overlap, client parity,
  two-install-entry-points, idempotency, brain-config-user-owned, per-consumer
  ownership of brain.aide, sync-as-only-mutation-surface, four-state vocabulary,
  substitution-partitioned, `.aide/config/` user-owned, CLI cold-start vs MCP
  agent-mediated. Half of these are brain-layer doctrine duplicating the root.
  The trunk's actual strategy is smaller: two delivery channels, progressive
  disclosure at delivery, no-overlap tool surface, client parity, idempotency.
  Brain-related decisions should live under a brain-layer parent.
  Traces to: `## Strategy` | Brevity Contract caps + "split, don't expand" + Sibling-redundancy
  Misalignment: spec-bloat, spec-gap
  Category: Brevity, Sibling-redundancy

- [ ] `## Bad examples` has 10+ scenarios with embedded code blocks containing
  exact prose, exact connector launcher invocations, exact key-name strings,
  exact JSON shapes. Cap is 2-3 brief examples; forbidden content rule forbids
  exact strings, file paths, and JSON shapes.
  Traces to: `## Bad examples` | Brevity Contract caps + Forbidden content
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity, Forbidden-content

- [ ] `## Good examples` includes a long block of exact orientation-section text
  in TypeScript-like prose form, exact MCP write-tool names, exact bullet-style
  reasoning the agent would follow. These are domain examples in name only;
  they're implementation example bytes for the bundled obsidian template — the
  same forbidden-content as the root.
  Traces to: `## Good examples` | Forbidden content (exact strings, exact bytes)
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Forbidden-content

- [ ] `## References` has 9 paragraph-length bullets; per the contract a bullet
  is "path + one-line description of what was drawn." Compress.
  Traces to: `## References` | Brevity Contract format
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity

### Carry-forward (prior alignment cycle, still relevant)

- [ ] `src/index.ts` lines 17–18, 148–196: `aide_info` and `aide_inspect` are
  registered and fully described in the handshake but are absent from
  `src/.aide` outcomes.desired[0]'s tool-surface enumeration. The spec states:
  "Each tool covers a distinct lifecycle phase with no overlap between them"
  and the undesired block warns against tools added because a responsibility
  "didn't quite fit" an existing tool. Neither aide_info nor aide_inspect is
  named in the spec, and no outcome justifies why they cannot be absorbed.
  This is the same drift class flagged in the prior cycle for `aide_upgrade`
  (since closed). The fix is to align the spec with what the server ships
  AFTER pruning the explicit-roster-enumeration pattern that keeps inviting
  this drift in the first place.
  Traces to: outcomes.desired (one tool per lifecycle phase, no overlap), outcomes.undesired (forbidden count-pinning and roster enumeration)
  Misalignment: spec-gap

## Retro

The trunk spec restates the parent's brain doctrine because brain-layer
narrative was put on the project root instead of being given its own
intermediate parent. The cleanup is structural: introduce a brain-layer
parent (e.g. push the brain doctrine into `.aide/brain/.aide` or into the
`src/service/` and `src/cli/` parents that own brain wiring code paths),
then prune both the root and this trunk to the doctrine that genuinely
applies project-wide.

The carry-forward tool-roster gap is a recurring drift class. The undesired
outcome that forbids count-pinning and roster enumeration is the right
defense; the desired outcome that enumerates tools nullifies it. Pick one:
keep the roster out of the spec entirely and let the actual server's
registered tool list be the authoritative surface, with QA verifying
"every lifecycle phase the methodology defines has at most one tool dedicated
to it" without naming any specific tool.

Recommended sequence: trim trunk after root is rewritten, in tandem with
the brain-layer-parent introduction. The aide_info/aide_inspect gap closes
when the roster enumeration is dropped from the trunk.
